* SETRECOVERY
* SET.RECOVERY verb: whether the IDE keeps a recovery copy of unsaved text (S.234 N4; SD Core does this with MODIFY.ACCOUNT name RECOVERY-ON | RECOVERY-OFF).
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 14 Nov 26 dm  New (S.234 N4, the Core-Solo parity audit of 14 Nov).  The IDE and !edit_recovery were carried from SD Core and read register field 19
*               (ACC$RECOVERY), but nothing in Solo could write it, so the copy could never be turned on: a restrictive default with no way to relax it.
*               Solo has no MODIFY.ACCOUNT and one account, so the switch has a verb of its own, as the home screen pins have SET.PINS.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SET.RECOVERY         says whether the IDE keeps a recovery copy of the account's unsaved editor text
*   SET.RECOVERY ON      keeps one: readable only by you, in ~/.local/state/sd/recovery/sduser/, offered back when the record is opened again
*   SET.RECOVERY OFF     keeps none (a copy already kept stays until it is offered and answered)
*
*   An administrator verb (ADMIN first).  OFF as delivered.  The switch is field 19 of the register record accounts/sduser (ACC$RECOVERY): ONLY 'yes'
*   is on, so a blank field, or a record with fewer fields, is off.  Takes effect at once: the editor reads the field each time it writes a copy.
*   Written to the audit trail in lower case.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program set.recovery
$catalog $setrecovery

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001) ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end

*  Solo's one account is always sduser (ruling 29); an internal session (the installer's) has another @logname, so the name is not read.
   acc.name = 'sduser'

   words = trim(@sentence)
   n.words = dcount(words, ' ')
   want = downcase(field(words, ' ', 2))
   if n.words > 2 or (n.words = 2 and want # 'on' and want # 'off') then stop sysmsg(14960)     ;* set.recovery {on | off}

   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'accounts' to acc.f else stop sysmsg(6003)
   readu acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else
      release acc.f, acc.name
      close acc.f
      stop sysmsg(6003)                                 ;* Account name is invalid
   end
   is.on = (upcase(trim(acc.rec<ACC$RECOVERY>)) = 'YES')

   if n.words = 1 then
      release acc.f, acc.name
      close acc.f
      @system.return.code = 0
      display sysmsg(11770, acc.name, (if is.on then 'yes' else 'no'))       ;* Account %1 keeps a recovery copy of unsaved editor text: %2
      stop
   end

   if is.on = (want = 'on') then
      release acc.f, acc.name
      close acc.f
      @system.return.code = 0
      display sysmsg(14961, acc.name, want)             ;* The recovery copy for %1 was already %2; nothing changed
      stop
   end

   acc.rec<ACC$RECOVERY> = (if want = 'on' then 'yes' else 'no')
   ok = @true
   write acc.rec to acc.f, acc.name on error ok = @false
   if not(ok) then release acc.f, acc.name
   close acc.f
   if not(ok) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(14962)                                ;* The recovery copy switch could not be written
   end

   @system.return.code = 0
   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'set.recovery ' : want : ' account=' : acc.name)
   display sysmsg(11770, acc.name, (if want = 'on' then 'yes' else 'no'))
   stop

end

* END-CODE
